1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,349
A torch glows with a battery.

2
00:00:02,349 --> 00:00:05,346
A wall clock ticks for
months on one small cell.

3
00:00:05,746 --> 00:00:08,873
Inside every cell, a
redox reaction is running.

4
00:00:09,323 --> 00:00:12,097
Aarav clips a battery
lamp onto his bicycle.

5
00:00:12,097 --> 00:00:15,202
He wonders where the
electricity really comes from.

6
00:00:15,552 --> 00:00:17,041
No wire from the wall.

7
00:00:17,041 --> 00:00:19,171
Just chemicals inside a small cell.

8
00:00:19,671 --> 00:00:23,394
By the end, you will see a redox
reaction push electrons through a wire.

9
00:00:24,044 --> 00:00:25,044
Start simple.

10
00:00:25,044 --> 00:00:28,269
Dip a zinc rod into blue
copper sulphate solution.

11
00:00:28,619 --> 00:00:31,889
After a while, reddish
brown copper coats the zinc.

12
00:00:31,889 --> 00:00:33,791
The blue colour slowly fades.

13
00:00:34,191 --> 00:00:37,910
Zinc gives electrons to copper
ions directly, right on the rod.

14
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:40,530
The energy only warms
the solution a little.

15
00:00:41,130 --> 00:00:45,658
Now let's split this reaction into
two beakers, on our molecule bench.

16
00:00:46,008 --> 00:00:48,608
Left beaker: a zinc rod in zinc sulphate.

17
00:00:48,608 --> 00:00:51,433
Right beaker: a copper
rod in copper sulphate.

18
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:55,838
Join the rods with a wire, and
the solutions with a salt bridge.

19
00:00:55,838 --> 00:00:58,326
A salt bridge is a tube of salt jelly.

20
00:00:58,326 --> 00:01:01,037
It lets ions move, and closes the circuit.

21
00:01:01,437 --> 00:01:03,081
Now zinc atoms give electrons.

22
00:01:03,081 --> 00:01:06,009
The electrons travel through
the wire to the copper rod.

23
00:01:06,009 --> 00:01:08,560
There, copper ions take
them and become copper.

24
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:11,441
Electrons flowing in a
wire are electricity.

25
00:01:11,441 --> 00:01:13,575
The meter reads one point one volts.

26
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:15,131
This is the Daniell cell.

27
00:01:15,731 --> 00:01:18,869
Each rod in its own solution
is called an electrode.

28
00:01:18,869 --> 00:01:21,625
Every electrode has its
own pull on electrons.

29
00:01:21,625 --> 00:01:23,772
We call it the electrode potential.

30
00:01:24,172 --> 00:01:28,836
When every solution is one molar and
the temperature is two ninety eight

31
00:01:28,836 --> 00:01:32,774
kelvin, it is the standard
electrode potential, E zero.

32
00:01:33,174 --> 00:01:35,652
But we cannot measure one electrode alone.

33
00:01:35,652 --> 00:01:38,739
Like height, we need a
zero level to measure from.

34
00:01:39,339 --> 00:01:42,623
That zero is the standard
hydrogen electrode.

35
00:01:42,623 --> 00:01:46,491
Hydrogen gas bubbles over
platinum in one molar acid.

36
00:01:46,491 --> 00:01:49,112
Its E zero is set at zero volts.

37
00:01:49,612 --> 00:01:51,112
Measure zinc against it.

38
00:01:51,112 --> 00:01:55,082
Zinc pushes electrons out, so zinc
gets minus point seven six volts.

39
00:01:55,832 --> 00:01:57,359
Measure copper against it.

40
00:01:57,359 --> 00:02:01,255
Copper pulls electrons in, so copper
gets plus point three four volts.

41
00:02:02,005 --> 00:02:06,468
The gap between them is point three
four minus minus point seven six.

42
00:02:06,468 --> 00:02:08,261
One point one zero volts.

43
00:02:08,261 --> 00:02:09,994
The Daniell cell voltage.

44
00:02:10,644 --> 00:02:13,171
Line up many electrodes by their E zero.

45
00:02:13,171 --> 00:02:15,587
This list is the electrochemical series.

46
00:02:15,987 --> 00:02:19,201
At the bottom, lithium,
minus three point zero five.

47
00:02:19,201 --> 00:02:21,358
It gives away electrons most easily.

48
00:02:21,358 --> 00:02:23,247
It is a strong reducing agent.

49
00:02:23,647 --> 00:02:26,654
At the top, fluorine, plus
two point eight seven.

50
00:02:26,654 --> 00:02:28,578
It grabs electrons most strongly.

51
00:02:28,578 --> 00:02:30,466
It is a strong oxidising agent.

52
00:02:30,866 --> 00:02:35,568
A metal with a more negative E zero
pushes out a metal with a more positive

53
00:02:35,568 --> 00:02:36,941
one from its salt.

54
00:02:36,941 --> 00:02:39,332
That is why zinc pushes out copper.

55
00:02:39,932 --> 00:02:42,536
Now compare zinc and copper side by side.

56
00:02:42,536 --> 00:02:44,456
Zinc, minus point seven six.

57
00:02:44,456 --> 00:02:45,748
It gives electrons.

58
00:02:46,148 --> 00:02:47,886
Copper, plus point three four.

59
00:02:47,886 --> 00:02:49,001
It takes electrons.

60
00:02:49,501 --> 00:02:50,615
Pause and think.

61
00:02:50,615 --> 00:02:52,853
Put a copper rod into zinc sulphate.

62
00:02:52,853 --> 00:02:54,765
Will copper push out the zinc?

63
00:02:57,965 --> 00:02:58,865
No.

64
00:02:58,345 --> 00:03:00,049
Copper has the higher E zero.

65
00:03:00,049 --> 00:03:02,556
It would rather take
electrons than give them.

66
00:03:02,556 --> 00:03:03,524
Nothing happens.

67
00:03:04,174 --> 00:03:05,113
Remember this.

68
00:03:05,113 --> 00:03:07,272
More negative E zero gives electrons.

69
00:03:07,272 --> 00:03:09,211
More positive E zero takes them.

70
00:03:09,911 --> 00:03:11,615
Now, one worked example.

71
00:03:11,615 --> 00:03:13,433
A cell of zinc and silver.

72
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:16,956
Silver has E zero plus
point eight zero volts.

73
00:03:16,956 --> 00:03:19,209
Zinc has minus point seven six.

74
00:03:19,709 --> 00:03:22,553
Zinc is more negative,
so zinc gives electrons.

75
00:03:22,553 --> 00:03:25,165
Zinc is the anode,
where oxidation happens.

76
00:03:25,915 --> 00:03:27,704
Silver ions take the electrons.

77
00:03:27,704 --> 00:03:30,363
Silver is the cathode,
where reduction happens.

78
00:03:31,113 --> 00:03:33,678
Cell voltage is cathode minus anode.

79
00:03:33,678 --> 00:03:36,849
Point eight zero minus
minus point seven six.

80
00:03:36,849 --> 00:03:38,765
One point five six volts.

81
00:03:39,415 --> 00:03:40,879
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:41,229 --> 00:03:43,213
The reaction uses two silver ions.

83
00:03:43,213 --> 00:03:46,881
So students double silver's E
zero, and write one point six zero.

84
00:03:47,281 --> 00:03:50,292
E zero does not change
with the number of ions.

85
00:03:50,292 --> 00:03:52,606
Never multiply it by the coefficient.

86
00:03:53,256 --> 00:03:54,700
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:58,480
Boards ask about the Daniell
cell and the hydrogen electrode.

88
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:03,017
Entrance tests ask which metal pushes
out which, or for a cell voltage.

89
00:04:03,617 --> 00:04:04,798
Exam level now.

90
00:04:04,798 --> 00:04:07,792
Iron has E zero minus
point four four volts.

91
00:04:07,792 --> 00:04:10,056
Copper has plus point three four.

92
00:04:10,056 --> 00:04:15,323
In a standard cell of these two, which
is the anode, and what is the voltage?

93
00:04:15,323 --> 00:04:16,554
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:21,954 --> 00:04:23,212
The answer is A.

95
00:04:23,212 --> 00:04:27,279
Iron is the anode, and the voltage
is point seven eight volts.

96
00:04:27,779 --> 00:04:30,578
Iron is more negative,
so iron gives electrons.

97
00:04:30,578 --> 00:04:31,802
Iron is the anode.

98
00:04:32,302 --> 00:04:33,700
Cathode minus anode.

99
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:36,613
Point three four minus
minus point four four.

100
00:04:37,113 --> 00:04:38,778
Point seven eight volts.

101
00:04:38,778 --> 00:04:41,014
C forgot the minus sign of iron.

102
00:04:41,614 --> 00:04:43,155
Here is your recap.

103
00:04:43,155 --> 00:04:44,739
Screenshot this card.

104
00:04:47,939 --> 00:04:50,125
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:04:50,525 --> 00:04:51,425
One.

106
00:04:51,005 --> 00:04:54,346
What is the E zero of the
standard hydrogen electrode?

107
00:04:55,146 --> 00:04:56,046
Two.

108
00:04:55,504 --> 00:04:58,327
In a Daniell cell, which way
do electrons flow in the wire?

109
00:04:59,127 --> 00:05:00,027
Three.

110
00:04:59,615 --> 00:05:01,916
Will copper push zinc
out of zinc sulphate?

111
00:05:02,816 --> 00:05:07,413
Next chapter: how do chemists find
out what a carbon compound is made of?

112
00:05:07,413 --> 00:05:11,745
We find out in organic chemistry,
some basic principles and techniques.
